<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Three Bishoprics</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1552">1552</a>–<a href="/years/1790">1790</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Trois-Évêchés</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Three Bishoprics were the cities and ecclesiastical principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a> occupied them in 1552 in alliance with German Protestant princes, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> formally ceded them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1648. They formed a French province until the Revolution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/three-bishoprics.jpg" alt="Three Bishoprics" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trois-Ev%C3%AAch%C3%A9s_in_France_(1789).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
